  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1229 PM MDT Mon May 25 2026\n\nACTIVE TODAY AND TOMORROW:\n\nCAM's are on track for a ramp up in active weather today as \nsoutherly flow around the SoCal low moves overhead. Whereas the \npast two days have seen scattered, mostly terrain-driven \nshowers, an uptick in moisture paired with a potentially more \nunstable environment should present itself over the next couple \ndays. This is resulting in more potent and widespread convective\ndevelopment. As far as today goes, moisture is good not great, \nwith PWAT's 120-150% of normal across the majority of the \nregion. Most of this is residing in southwestern Colorado and \nsoutheastern Utah, but even in the 18Z GJT sounding we can see \nsome surface moisture beginning to creep in. The caveat \nregarding storm development today will be how much instability \nis able to form. Dense cloud cover from the incoming plume of \nmoisture from the south will hamper much convective development \nacross the CWA. Meanwhile, across the north where clearer skies \nwill support stronger CAPE, moisture will be dragging behind. A \n\"sweet spot\" may exist across the central CWA (around I-70 and \nUS-50), but even so, forecast soundings for this afternoon are \nunimpressive in the overall dynamics, so the anticipation is \nthat we won't see much more than small hail and gusty outflow \nwinds from thunderstorms.\n\nThe story is similar tomorrow, though the moisture plume will \nhave fully enveloped the region by this point with pockets of \nPWAT's ~200% of normal settling in. Overall, precipitation will \nbecome more widespread. But again, like today, dense morning \ncloud cover will be the inhibiting factor to stronger \nconvection. However, with 500mb winds picking up in the \nafternoon, deep shear is expected to develop on Tuesday, \nsomething not present today. This alone could be enough to \nsupport a few sub-severe to marginally severe hail producers \ntomorrow afternoon, but unless we see a stronger CAPE develop, \nthese more potent storms will be few and far between. Additional\nhi-res model guidance and day-of soundings will provide a \nclearer picture on the full potential of Tuesday's storms, so \nstay tuned over the next 24 hours for more information.\n\nTo note, the more favorable moisture profile over the next \ncouple days could lead to some storms being capable of dropping \nheavy rainfall. This shouldn't pose much of a flash flooding \nthreat for most areas, unless they pass over one of our many \nburn scars. This will be something we keep a close eye on as the\nsystem passes through.\n\nWEDNESDAY AND BEYOND:\n\nAs the subsequent PacNW low descends into the Intermountain \nWest and the closed low driving the next couple days' weather \ngets reabsorbed into this flow, drier air will advect back into \nthe region. Moisture will be near-normal throughout the rest of \nthe week as result, which will be enough to prompt daily \norographic showers, but little else in the way of rainfall.",
